\chapter{User Codecs and Abstract Byte I/O}
\label{ch:codecs-io}

Every serious program has to move bytes, and every serious program has to
decide what those bytes mean. The two decisions are independent, and the
worst design mistake in a library is to fuse them. A toolkit that ships
its own file format has decided the syntax for everyone who uses it, and a
toolkit that ships its own file abstraction has decided where the bytes
live. DSLtk makes neither decision. It defines \emph{protocols}: a small
set of named operations that a user-supplied type must provide, and thin
adapters that check the protocol and forward to it.

The result is a boundary that is unusually clean. The library never opens
a file, never allocates a socket, never chooses an encoding, and never
owns the object that does the work. What it does provide is a place where
those choices can be made once and reused, and a set of guarantees---about
short transfers, about premature end of file, about the meaning of a zero
count---that hold no matter what the underlying transport happens to be.

This chapter describes the two protocols DSLtk offers in this area. The
first is the codec protocol, which is about \emph{syntax}: how a value of
type \code{T} becomes a sequence of characters or bytes and back again.
The second is the byte-I/O protocol, which is about \emph{transport}: how
bytes are moved. A user-supplied backend can implement either, both, or
neither, and the same backend can be used with a file, a socket, an
in-memory buffer, or a test double.

\section{Why the library ships no file system}

The decision not to provide a file or socket implementation is deliberate,
and it is worth defending, because it is the kind of decision that looks
like an omission until one tries to build a real program on top of it.

A library that ships \code{read_file} has made at least four choices on
the user's behalf: that files are the unit of storage, that the
implementation is a thin wrapper over the platform's own file API, that
errors are reported as exceptions, and that the lifetime of the resulting
buffer is managed by the library. Every one of those choices is
reasonable, and every one of them is wrong for somebody. A program that
reads from a pipe, a network socket, an embedded flash region, or a
compressed archive needs the same protocol without the file assumption. A
program that must survive a truncated input needs error reporting that is
not an exception. A program that embeds its configuration in a read-only
memory-mapped page needs a different lifetime story.

Providing the protocol instead of an implementation costs the library very
little---a concept and a forwarding struct---and it moves every one of
those decisions to the place where the answers are known. The price the
user pays is a few lines of adapter code, and the price is worth paying
because the adapter code is written once, in one place, and can then be
tested against a deterministic in-memory backend.

\section{The codec protocol}

The codec protocol is expressed by two concepts and realised by two
adapters. The first concept, \api{SerializerBackend}, describes a type
\code{B} that can serialise a value of type \code{T} into an output of
type \code{Output}. The second, \api{DeserializerBackend}, describes a
type \code{B} that can deserialise a value of type \code{T} from an input
of type \code{Input} (\texttt{DSLtk.hpp}, lines 9862 and 9867):

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption=The two codec concepts.,label=lst:codec-concepts]
template <class B, class T, class Output>
concept SerializerBackend
    = requires (B &backend, const T &value, Output &output) {
        backend.serialize (value, output);
      };

template <class B, class T, class Input>
concept DeserializerBackend = requires (B &backend, Input &input) {
  backend.template deserialize<T> (input);
};
\end{lstlisting}

The asymmetry between the two is not an accident. Serialisation is a sink:
the value goes in and the output is modified, so there is nothing to
return and the concept says nothing about a return type. Deserialisation
is a source: the input is consumed and a value comes out, so the
concept---and, more importantly, the adapter---must be able to name that
value's type, which is exactly why the call is spelled
\code{deserialize<T>}.

The adapters are correspondingly thin:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption=The codec adapters. Both hold a reference and forward with \code{decltype(auto)}.,label=lst:codec-adapters]
template <class Backend> struct Serializer
{
  Backend &backend;
  explicit Serializer (Backend &backend) : backend (backend) {}
  template <class T, class Output>
    requires SerializerBackend<Backend, T, Output>
  decltype (auto)
  serialize (const T &value, Output &output)
  {
    return backend.serialize (value, output);
  }
};

template <class Backend> struct Deserializer
{
  Backend &backend;
  explicit Deserializer (Backend &backend) : backend (backend) {}
  template <class T, class Input>
    requires DeserializerBackend<Backend, T, Input>
  decltype (auto)
  deserialize (Input &input)
  {
    return backend.template deserialize<T> (input);
  }
};
\end{lstlisting}

Four properties of these twelve lines carry most of the design.

First, the adapter \emph{borrows}. The member is \code{Backend &backend},
not \code{Backend backend} and not \code{std::unique_ptr<Backend>}. The
library never owns a backend and never chooses when it is destroyed. The
consequence for the user is a lifetime obligation: the backend must
outlive the adapter, and the adapter must not outlive the backend. In
exchange, the adapter is trivially copyable, has no allocation, and costs
nothing to pass around.

Second, the adapter forwards the return type exactly. The return type of
\code{serialize} and of \code{deserialize} is \code{decltype (auto)},
which deduces the backend's own return type rather than a fixed one. A
backend whose \code{serialize} returns \code{bool} gives an adapter that
returns \code{bool}; a backend whose \code{deserialize} returns a
\code{dsl::Result<int, std::string>} gives an adapter that returns that
result type; a backend that returns a reference gives an adapter that
returns a reference. The library imposes no error convention at all,
because the error convention is the backend's business.

Third, the output type is a free parameter. \code{Output} and \code{Input}
are deduced at the call site, not fixed by the adapter. The same backend
can therefore serialise into a \code{std::string} and into a
\code{std::vector<std::byte>} provided it offers the appropriate
overloads, and the adapter never has to know which.

Fourth, the constraints are on the member function, not on the class. A
\code{Serializer} can be instantiated for a backend that cannot serialise
anything at all; the error appears only when the incompatible overload is
named, and it appears as a concept failure rather than as a hard error
inside a template body.

