\section{Two protocols, two granularities}

It is worth pausing on the difference between the two protocols, because
they answer questions at different levels and a reader who expects them to
be symmetrical will be confused by the asymmetry of the concepts.

The codec protocol is about the \emph{contents} of a message. Its unit of
work is a value of a particular type, and its direction is semantic: a
value of type \code{T} becomes bytes, and bytes become a value of type
\code{T}. The transport is invisible to it. A codec that writes a
length-prefixed record into a \code{std::string} is a perfectly good
codec whether that string is later written to a file, sent over a socket,
or held in memory for a test.

The byte protocol is about the \emph{movement} of bytes. Its unit of work
is a span, and its direction is mechanical: bytes go into a buffer, bytes
come out of a buffer. The contents are invisible to it. The adapter has
no idea whether the bytes it moves are a text document, a compressed
archive, or the output of a codec.

The two meet at a seam that the user owns. A codec that wants to write to
a file must first produce something the byte protocol can move; a codec
that wants to read from a socket must consume something the byte protocol
produced. The seam is usually a pair of small conversion functions, as in
the worked example below, and it is usually the only place in the program
where the two vocabularies touch.

This division has a practical consequence for testing. The byte protocol
can be tested against a backend that is nothing but a
\code{std::vector<std::byte>}, with no file system and no timing
behaviour, and the codec can be tested against a \code{std::string} with
no I/O at all. Because the adapters are the only code that knows about
both, a program that keeps its seam narrow keeps most of its logic
testable without a disk.

\section{The byte-I/O protocol}

The byte protocol is a single concept, \api{IOBackend}
(\texttt{DSLtk.hpp}, line 9895):

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption=The byte-I/O concept.,label=lst:io-concept]
template <class B>
concept IOBackend = requires (B &backend, std::span<std::byte> out,
                              std::span<const std::byte> in) {
  { backend.read (out) } -> std::convertible_to<std::size_t>;
  { backend.write (in) } -> std::convertible_to<std::size_t>;
};
\end{lstlisting}

The contract is stated in the header's own comment and it is short enough
to quote in full. Read and write return the number of bytes transferred. A
zero read means end of file. Short reads and short writes are supported.
Seeking and telling are optional backend operations.

Short transfers being supported is the load-bearing part of the contract.
A backend that reads from a network connection, from a pipe, or from a
compressed stream will frequently be able to satisfy only part of a
request, and a protocol that demanded an exact transfer would have to
either loop inside the backend or fail. The library chooses the former
philosophy for the adapter and the latter for the concept: the concept
says nothing about how much a single call must move, and the adapter
supplies the looping.

The adapter is \api{IO}, and unlike the codec adapters it is constrained at
the class level, because every instance of it must be able to read and
write:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption=The byte-I/O adapter.,label=lst:io-adapter]
template <IOBackend Backend> struct IO
{
  Backend &backend;
  explicit IO (Backend &backend) : backend (backend) {}

  std::size_t read (std::span<std::byte> bytes)
  {
    auto n = static_cast<std::size_t> (backend.read (bytes));
    if (n > bytes.size ())
      throw std::runtime_error ("I/O backend returned an invalid read count");
    return n;
  }

  std::size_t write (std::span<const std::byte> bytes)
  {
    auto n = static_cast<std::size_t> (backend.write (bytes));
    if (n > bytes.size ())
      throw std::runtime_error ("I/O backend returned an invalid write count");
    return n;
  }

  void read_exact (std::span<std::byte> bytes)
  {
    while (!bytes.empty ())
      {
        auto n = read (bytes);
        if (!n)
          throw std::runtime_error ("unexpected I/O EOF");
        bytes = bytes.subspan (n);
      }
  }

  void write_all (std::span<const std::byte> bytes)
  {
    while (!bytes.empty ())
      {
        auto n = write (bytes);
        if (!n)
          throw std::runtime_error ("I/O write made no progress");
        bytes = bytes.subspan (n);
      }
  }

  void seek (std::size_t position)
    requires requires { backend.seek (position); }
  {
    backend.seek (position);
  }

  auto tell ()
    requires requires { backend.tell (); }
  {
    return backend.tell ();
  }
};
\end{lstlisting}

Several behaviours are worth drawing out.

The count returned by a backend is \emph{checked}, not trusted. If a
backend claims to have transferred more bytes than the buffer holds, the
adapter throws \code{std::runtime_error} whose message is
\emph{I/O backend returned an invalid read count} or the corresponding write message. The check also catches
the common failure of reporting an error with a negative number: the
adapter casts the result to \code{std::size_t} first, so a
\code{-1} becomes an enormous unsigned value and is rejected by the same
comparison. A backend that wants to report an error should throw, not
return a sentinel.

\code{read_exact} and \code{write_all} are the two convenience
operations, and their names are exact. The first fills the whole span,
or throws a \code{std::runtime_error} whose message is \emph{unexpected
I/O EOF} the moment a read returns zero. The second writes the whole
span, or throws a \code{std::runtime_error} whose message is \emph{I/O
write made no progress} when a write returns zero. Neither name is
\code{read_all} and neither position accessor is \code{position}; the
library's spelling is \code{read_exact} and \code{tell}.

\begin{warning}[A note on naming]
Several familiar names are absent from this API by design. There is no
\code{read_all}, because the operation that reads an entire stream until
end of file is a policy decision---how much memory to commit, whether to
stop at a limit---and the library declines to make it. There is no
\code{position}, because the accessor is named \code{tell}, matching the
vocabulary of the C and POSIX libraries that most readers already know.
And there is no \code{read_all} counterpart for writing, because
\code{write_all} is the one that is actually needed: a partial write that
is silently ignored is a data-corruption bug waiting to happen.
\end{warning}

\begin{notebox}[Writing a backend that reports errors]
The adapter's count checks catch two mistakes and only two: a count that
exceeds the buffer, and a negative count. They cannot catch a backend
that returns a perfectly plausible number for an operation that actually
failed. A backend that owns a file, for instance, must decide what to do
when the disk is full. It has three reasonable options, and the library
supports all three without comment. It can throw, in which case the
exception propagates through the adapter untouched. It can return a
short count, in which case \code{write_all} will retry the remainder and
the failure will surface as a repeated short write, or eventually as
\emph{I/O write made no progress} if the backend keeps returning zero. Or
it can return a distinguished value, such as a count of
\code{std::numeric_limits<std::size_t>::max()}, which the adapter
rejects as an invalid count. What the backend must not do is return a
short count on a permanent failure and expect the adapter to distinguish
it from an ordinary short write; the adapter has no way to tell them
apart, and pretending otherwise is the one mistake the protocol cannot
absorb.
\end{notebox}

Both convenience operations take their span by value and reslice it with
\code{subspan}, so the caller's span is never modified. An empty span is a
no-op in both cases, and in particular \code{read_exact} on an empty span
never throws, even at end of file.

Seeking and telling are \emph{conditionally present}. The two member
functions carry a nested \code{requires} constraint, and that constraint
checks that the backend itself offers a \code{seek} taking a
\code{std::size_t}. The consequence is that \code{IO<Backend>::seek}
exists only when the backend has such a member, and
\code{IO<Backend>::tell} exists only when the backend has a
zero-argument \code{tell}. A program that wants random access can say so by requiring
it, and a program that must work with a streaming backend will find at
compile time that the operation is not there. This is the concrete payoff
of making the optional operations genuinely optional rather than merely
documenting them as such.

\begin{pseudocode}[Read exactly N bytes]
\textbf{function} read\_exact (io, bytes)\\
\quad while bytes is not empty do\\
\qquad n = io.read (bytes)\\
\qquad if n is zero then\\
\qquad\qquad raise \emph{unexpected I/O EOF}\\
\qquad bytes = bytes.subspan (n)\\
\quad return
\end{pseudocode}

