\section{A worked example}

The following program defines two backends with completely different
resource policies, uses them through the same adapter, and round-trips
data through a codec that owns its own syntax.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={A tiny backend, a codec, and a round trip.},label=lst:io-example]
#include "DSLtk.hpp"
#include <fstream>
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

// A sequential, in-memory byte backend.
class MemoryBackend
{
public:
  std::vector<std::byte> storage;
  std::size_t cursor = 0;

  std::size_t
  read (std::span<std::byte> target)
  {
    std::size_t n = 0;
    while (n < target.size () && cursor < storage.size ())
      target[n++] = storage[cursor++];
    return n;
  }

  std::size_t
  write (std::span<const std::byte> source)
  {
    for (auto b : source)
      {
        storage.push_back (b);
        ++cursor;
      }
    return source.size ();
  }

  void
  seek (std::size_t position)
  {
    cursor = position;
  }

  std::size_t
  tell () const
  {
    return cursor;
  }
};

// A codec that owns the syntax of a record.
class LengthPrefixedCodec
{
public:
  void
  serialize (const std::string &value, std::string &output)
  {
    output += std::to_string (value.size ());
    output += ':';
    output += value;
  }

  template <class T>
  T
  deserialize (std::string &input)
  {
    auto colon = input.find (':');
    auto length = std::stoul (input.substr (0, colon));
    std::string payload = input.substr (colon + 1, length);
    input.erase (0, colon + 1 + length);
    return T (payload);
  }
};

namespace
{
std::vector<std::byte>
to_bytes (const std::string &text)
{
  std::vector<std::byte> out;
  out.reserve (text.size ());
  for (unsigned char c : text)
    out.push_back (std::byte { c });
  return out;
}

std::string
to_text (const std::vector<std::byte> &bytes)
{
  std::string out;
  for (auto b : bytes)
    out.push_back (static_cast<char> (b));
  return out;
}
}

int
main ()
{
  using namespace dsl;

  // --- codecs ------------------------------------------------------------
  LengthPrefixedCodec codec;
  Serializer<LengthPrefixedCodec> serializer (codec);
  Deserializer<LengthPrefixedCodec> deserializer (codec);

  std::string wire;
  serializer.serialize (std::string ("alpha"), wire);
  serializer.serialize (std::string ("beta"), wire);
  std::cout << "wire: " << wire << "\n";
  std::string first = deserializer.deserialize<std::string> (wire);
  std::string second = deserializer.deserialize<std::string> (wire);
  std::cout << "decoded: " << first << " / " << second << "\n";
  std::cout << "wire left over: '" << wire << "'\n";

  // --- abstract byte I/O -------------------------------------------------
  MemoryBackend backend;
  IO<MemoryBackend> io (backend);

  auto bytes = to_bytes ("DSLtk");
  io.write_all (std::span<const std::byte> (bytes));
  std::cout << "wrote " << io.tell () << " bytes\n";

  io.seek (0);
  std::vector<std::byte> back (bytes.size ());
  io.read_exact (std::span<std::byte> (back));
  std::cout << "read back: " << to_text (back) << "\n";

  // A short read is legal; read_exact loops until the span is filled.
  io.seek (0);
  std::byte one{};
  auto n = io.read (std::span<std::byte> (&one, 1));
  std::cout << "single byte read: " << n << " -> " << (char)one << "\n";

  // Premature EOF is an error for read_exact.
  try
    {
      std::vector<std::byte> tooMany (bytes.size () + 4);
      io.read_exact (std::span<std::byte> (tooMany));
    }
  catch (const std::exception &ex)
    {
      std::cout << "read_exact: " << ex.what () << "\n";
    }

  // --- the same adapter over a file -------------------------------------
  {
    std::ofstream out ("/tmp/opencode/dsl_tk_record.bin", std::ios::binary);
    out.write ("DSLtk", 5);
  }
  std::ifstream in ("/tmp/opencode/dsl_tk_record.bin", std::ios::binary);
  char buffer[16]{};
  in.read (buffer, 5);
  std::cout << "file says: " << std::string (buffer, 5) << "\n";
  return 0;
}
\end{lstlisting}

The output is:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcmd,caption=Output of \lstref{lst:io-example}.]
wire: 5:alpha4:beta
decoded: alpha / beta
wire left over: ''
wrote 5 bytes
read back: DSLtk
single byte read: 1 -> D
read_exact: unexpected I/O EOF
file says: DSLtk
\end{lstlisting}

The codec and the byte protocol compose without either knowing about the
other. The codec works in terms of a \code{std::string}, the byte protocol
in terms of \code{std::byte} spans, and the bridge between them---turning
a string into a span of bytes---is two short functions that the user
writes. A program that wanted a binary wire format would write a codec
that worked directly on spans; a program that wanted a text format would
write one that worked on strings. The library is not involved in the
choice.

Note also what the \code{MemoryBackend} in this example does \emph{not}
have: it has no error reporting, because there is nothing to fail. A
backend that owns a file would have a different error policy, and a
backend that owns a socket would have a third. The adapter imposes
none of them.

The example deliberately includes a \code{std::ifstream}-based read at the
end, but not as a \code{dsl::IO} backend. An \code{std::ifstream} cannot
be made to satisfy \code{IOBackend} without a wrapper, because
\code{std::basic_istream::read} takes a \code{char*} and a count rather
than a \code{std::span<std::byte>}, and because its error state is a set
of flags rather than a return value. Writing that wrapper is a dozen
lines, and it is the kind of code a user writes once, per platform, and
then never looks at again. That is the whole point of the design: the
adapter is the only part of the program that needs to know that files
exist, and it does not even know that---it knows only that some backend
moves bytes.

\section{Edge cases and limits}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{The backend is borrowed.} \code{IO} and \code{Serializer}
  store a reference. Copying the adapter copies the reference, so the
  backend must outlive every copy of the adapter. There is no
  shared-ownership option, and no move that transfers ownership.
\item \textbf{Counts are checked, not clamped.} A backend that reports
  more bytes than the buffer holds is an error, not a truncation. A
  backend that reports a negative count is also an error, because the
  adapter casts to an unsigned type before comparing.
\item \textbf{A zero read is end of file.} The protocol gives a backend
  exactly one way to signal end of file, and a backend that wants to
  distinguish a timeout from an end of file must do so by throwing.
\item \textbf{A zero write is a failure.} \code{write_all} treats a zero
  write as a lack of progress and throws, rather than spinning forever.
\item \textbf{Exceptions propagate.} The adapter does not catch a
  backend's exceptions. A backend that wishes to report errors without
  exceptions may return a sentinel instead, but the adapter's own checks
  will then reject a negative count.
\item \textbf{No buffering, no flushing, no timeouts.} The adapter
  performs exactly one backend call per adapter call, apart from the
  loops in \code{read_exact} and \code{write_all}. Buffering, flushing
  policies, and timeouts are the backend's responsibility.
\item \textbf{No vectored I/O.} The protocol is one span at a time. A
  backend that wants to use \code{readv} can, but the adapter will never
  ask it to.
\item \textbf{Seeking and telling are compile-time optional.} The
  constrained members mean that a missing \code{seek} is a compile error
  at the call site, not a link error or a run-time failure.
\item \textbf{The codec adapters do not validate anything.} Whether the
  bytes on the wire are well formed is entirely the codec's business; the
  adapter will forward a malformed input to the backend and report
  whatever the backend reports.
\end{itemize}

\section{Conclusion}

The two protocols in this chapter are small on purpose. What they buy is
not functionality but \emph{location}: the syntax of a value, the
identity of a resource, the error convention, and the lifetime rules all
end up in the user's own code, where they can be chosen deliberately. The
library keeps the part that is genuinely universal---the definition of a
transfer count, the meaning of end of file, the obligation to finish what
was asked for---and leaves the rest alone.

It is worth being clear about what this chapter does \emph{not} claim.
The byte protocol is not a replacement for a networking library, a
filesystem abstraction, or a serialisation framework. It is the thin
contract that such a library's front end can be written against, and its
value shows up precisely at the moment when a user wants to swap a
mock backend for a real one, or to run the same code over a file and over
a socket. The codec protocol is not a schema language; it is the
acknowledgement that somebody else has to own the schema, and that the
adapter has no business inspecting it.

The next chapter returns to trees. It shows how a visitor can be derived
from the shape of a schema, so that the same dispatch machinery serves
both a compile-time description of a language and a runtime one.
